## Identifier Conventions

The `{{ID_PREFIX}}-*` namespace covers every identifier in the project, not only requirements — this is the canonical registry for all of them, to avoid collisions between requirement, question, decision, and work-item IDs.

Current ID forms:

- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-FUN-NNN`: functional requirement.
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-NFR-NNN`: non-functional requirement.
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-AC-NNN`: acceptance criterion.
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-Q-NNN`: open question, tracked in [../../open/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md](../../open/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md).
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-D-NNN`: open decision, tracked in [../../open/OPEN_DECISIONS.md](../../open/OPEN_DECISIONS.md).
- `{{ID_PREFIX}}-W-NNN`: open work item, tracked in [../../open/OPEN_WORK.md](../../open/OPEN_WORK.md).

Component meanings:

- `{{ID_PREFIX}}`: project namespace; reduces collision risk and makes search/renumbering practical.
- `FUN` / `NFR`: functional or non-functional requirement.
- `AC`: acceptance criterion — a testable condition proving a requirement is satisfied.
- `Q` / `D` / `W`: open question, open decision, or open work item — see `open/`.
- `NNN`: three-digit, zero-padded sequence number.

Recommended search pattern:

```regex
\b{{ID_PREFIX}}-(FUN|NFR|AC|Q|D|W)-[0-9]{3}\b
```

All `{{ID_PREFIX}}-*` IDs are internal, including `Q`/`D`/`W`. They must never appear in user-facing text (CLI help, error messages, README, USAGE) — only in docstrings, comments, tests, commit messages, and `docs/`. When tempted to cite an ID in user-facing text, state the reason instead — the reason tells the user something; the ID tells them nothing.

### External / Team IDs

If a broader team maintains separate requirement numbers or ticket IDs, do not force this project's IDs to match prematurely. Maintain a mapping table (workspace ID, external ID, source system, relationship type, confidence, notes) instead of renumbering.

